{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794385794386,"sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","part":{"id":"prt_1c9ba5535001mkZfVlDIMGKcZY","messageID":"msg_1c9ba029e001Ut4PDAZ0YknuVf","sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794385794387,"sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","part":{"id":"prt_1c9ba5539001f5c1kJMWDAEBgM","messageID":"msg_1c9ba029e001Ut4PDAZ0YknuVf","sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","type":"text","text":"<think>The lock file has hashes, so it requires `--require-hashes`. The Docker build uses `pip install -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock` — with `-c` (constraints), pip shouldn't require hashes... but the error says \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode\". Hmm, pip enables require-hashes when *any* requirement in the constraints file has a hash? Actually, pip's `--require-hashes` is auto-enabled if any requirement (including constraints?) has a hash. Per pip docs: \"If any hash is present in the requirements file, hashes are required for all requirements\". Constraints files with hashes... pip's behavior: constraints don't trigger require-hashes. Hmm, but the error occurred.\n\nWait — maybe pip treats constraints hashes as enabling require-hashes mode. Let me look at the lock file format.\n\nActually the error message says \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements\" and lists flask==3.1.3 as missing a hash — because the constraint provides the hash but the requirement line in requirements.txt doesn't have one. In require-hashes mode, every requirement needs its own hash.\n\nSo how does the Docker build succeed? `pip install -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock` — in the Docker build, the same error would occur... unless pip only auto-enables require-hashes when the *requirements* file has hashes. Let me check the lock file contents: it's `requirements.lock` (a constraints file). Let me look at its head.\n\nHmm, pip's rule (from pip source): in `pip/_internal/req/req_file.py`, `RequirementsFile.parse` → for constraint files, hashes are ignored? Let me check pip's behavior: In pip, `require_hashes` is determined by `finder.require_hashes` which is set in `parse_requirements`:\n```python\n        for req_file in req_files:\n            ...\n            if req_file.is_constraint:\n                ...\n```\nActually in pip's `req_file.py`:\n```python\n    def parse_requirements(...):\n        ...\n```\nand in `pip/_internal/commands/install.py` / `resolution/resolvelib`:\n```python\n    def _get_requirement_set(...)\n```\nHmm. In `pip/_internal/index/collector.py`? Let me recall `pip/_internal/req/__init__.py`:\n```python\ndef check_invalid_constraint_type(req): ...\n```\nThere's a known behavior: \"pip install --require-hashes\" auto-enabled when the *requirements file* contains hashes; constraints files' hashes also count? The error we saw proves that constraints hashes DO trigger require-hashes mode in this pip version (pip 25.3).\n\nBut then how does the Docker build work? Maybe it doesn't need to — maybe the Docker build in the grading environment fails the same way?! That can't be; the base project must build.\n\nWait — maybe pip's behavior differs: in the Docker image, pip version is from python:3.13-slim at the time of image creation (pip ~25.x). The behavior should be the same...\n\nHmm, let me look at the requirements.lock file header — maybe it's a full requirements file with hashes (not just constraints). The Docker command passes it with `-c`, so pip treats it as constraints. In require-hashes mode, constraints' hashes can't satisfy requirement hashes → error.\n\nUnless... the lock file's hashes are for a different set. Let me inspect the lock file to understand.\n\nActually wait. Let me re-read the pip error: \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had.\"\n\nThis is the classic error when using `-r requirements.txt` with hashes in a constraints file. Hmm, but actually I recall pip's rule precisely:\n\nFrom pip source (`pip/_internal/req/req_file.py`):\n```python\nclass ParsedRequirement:\n    ...\nclass RequirementsFile:\n    ...\n```\nand in `pip/_internal/commands/install.py`:\n```python\n    def get_requirements(...):\n        ...\n        for req_file in args.constraints:\n            parsed_requirements.extend(\n                self._parse_file(...)  # parse as constraints\n            )\n```\nThe require_hashes flag comes from `finder`:\n```python\nclass PackageFinder:\n    def __init__(self, ..., require_hashes=False, ...)\n```\nand in `pip/_internal/commands/install.py`:\n```python\n        if options.require_hashes:\n            ...\n```\nHmm, where does auto-enable happen? In `pip/_internal/req/req_file.py`:\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def parse_requirements(cls, filename, session, finder=None, options=None, constraint=False):\n        ...\n```\nand\n```python\ndef handle_line(...):\n    ...\n```\nI recall `pip/_internal/resolution/resolvelib/factory.py`:\n```python\n    def _make_requirement_from_install_req(...)\n```\nHmm.\n\nActually, the auto-enable is in `pip/_internal/req/req_file.py`:\n```python\n        if finder:\n            for req in requirements:\n                if req.is_editable: ...\n                elif req.hashes:\n                    finder.require_hashes = True\n```\nYes! I'm fairly confident: `finder.require_hashes = True` is set when a *parsed requirement* has hashes — and for constraint files, the parsed requirements are returned with `is_constraint` markers but their `hashes` are still set → triggering require_hashes mode.\n\nHmm, but in the Docker build, the same would happen. Unless the lock file format is such that the hashes are in the same file as the requirements (i.e., requirements.lock contains full requirement lines with hashes, and requirements.txt is a subset). Then `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` → the constraints' hashes trigger require_hashes → error, unless requirements.txt lines ALSO have hashes.\n\nLet me look at the actual lock file to see its format. Maybe the lock file lines have hashes and the requirements.txt entries... no, requirements.txt has plain `flask==3.1.3`.\n\nLet me examine the lock file.\n\nIf the Docker build would fail, that's a pre-existing problem in the scaffold — but the task says the app is built from the Dockerfile in the workspace root, so it must work. Perhaps the grading environment's pip version behaves differently (older pip auto-enables require_hashes only for the requirements file, not constraints). Let me check pip 25.3's actual behavior... The error I got is from pip 25.3.1 in this sandbox.\n\nHmm! But wait: maybe the difference is `--require-hashes` auto-enable happens only when hashes are found in a *non-constraint* file... but I got the error, so with this pip version, constraints DO trigger it.\n\nLet me check the lock file content first.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794385794361,"end":1794385794381}}}
