{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794385805754,"sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","part":{"id":"prt_1c9ba819e0010H98meb2Z85zj2","messageID":"msg_1c9ba557a001DfY9mGCD68zBGn","sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794385805754,"sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","part":{"id":"prt_1c9ba81a1001jeaWqLmwI5bUDM","messageID":"msg_1c9ba557a001DfY9mGCD68zBGn","sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","type":"text","text":"<think>The lock file is a pip-compile output with hashes. The Docker build runs:\n```\npip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock\n```\nWith pip 25.x this errors with require-hashes mode (as we saw). Hmm, but wait — pip's require-hashes auto-enable: in `pip/_internal/req/req_file.py`:\n\n```python\n    def parse_requirements(...):\n        ...\n        for line in lines:\n            ...\n```\nAnd in `pip/_internal/req/__init__.py`:\n```python\ndef parse_requirements(...)\n```\nHmm, the auto-enable happens in `pip/_internal/commands/install.py`? Let me actually check pip's source in this sandbox to see where `require_hashes` is set from constraint files.\n\nActually, pip's documented behavior: \"If you have hashes in a constraints file, pip will not enable require-hashes mode\" — hmm. Let me check the actual pip source.\n\nRegardless of the exact mechanism, the observed behavior in this sandbox's pip 25.3.1 is an error. But the Docker image build might use a different pip version (python:3.13-slim image's pip version at build time, likely 25.x too). So the Docker build might fail!\n\nHmm, but this is the provided scaffold, and the task states the app is built from this Dockerfile. If the Docker build fails due to pip's require-hashes behavior, that's a pre-existing issue that I should fix (the task says \"build the app on top of it\" — a broken build would fail the evaluation).\n\nWait, let me check pip's source to see the exact rule. In pip's `req_file.py`:\n\n```python\nclass ParsedRequirement:\n    ...\n    def __init__(self, requirement, is_editable, comes_from, constraint, options=None, line_source=None):\n```\n\nand\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def parse_requirements(cls, filename, session, finder=None, options=None, constraint=False):\n        ...\n        for line in lines:\n            ...\n            if req.is_editable:\n                ...\n            if finder:\n                if req.is_editable:\n                    ...\n                elif req.hashes:\n                    finder.require_hashes = True\n```\n\nHmm, in older pip versions this was:\n```python\n            if finder:\n                if req.is_editable:\n                    ...\n                else:\n                    for hash in req.hashes: ...\n```\nLet me look at the installed pip source directly.\n\nLet me check `/usr/lib/python3/dist-packages/pip/_internal/req/req_file.py`.\n\nActually, in pip 23+, the code is:\n\n```python\n    def parse_requirements(\n        self,\n        filename: str,\n        session: PipSession,\n        finder: PackageFinder,\n        options: Optional[Values] = None,\n        constraint: bool = False,\n    ) -> Iterator[InstallRequirement]:\n        \"\"\"Parse a requirements file and yield the parsed requirements.\"\"\"\n        ...\n        for parsed_req in ...:\n            yield parsed_req\n```\nand inside `handle_line`:\n```python\n        if req.is_editable:\n            ...\n        elif req comes from a constraint file: ...\n```\n\nLet me just grep the installed pip.\n\nThe key question: does the Docker build fail? I can simulate exactly the Docker command with the same pip version? The sandbox pip is 25.3.1 (Ubuntu system pip). The Docker image python:3.13-slim has pip installed (probably 25.0-25.2). Behavior likely the same.\n\nLet me check pip's source for where require_hashes is auto-enabled.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794385805729,"end":1794385805750}}}
