{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794385912177,"sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","part":{"id":"prt_1c9bc21540016nidqO5GkAJnxE","messageID":"msg_1c9bc0a19001HqCyQDsn6u5lai","sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794385912177,"sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","part":{"id":"prt_1c9bc2157001mAJ8cM0yqHhIC5","messageID":"msg_1c9bc0a19001HqCyQDsn6u5lai","sessionID":"ses_e364b2c5fffeewyZdGbyeKVnGO","type":"text","text":"<think>\"No module named 'expat'\" — the venv is missing the pyexpat module?! Python 3.13 slim has expat built in normally. The venv was created from the system python3.13 (Ubuntu) — hmm, \"No module named 'expat'\" suggests the venv's python lacks the _elementtree/expat C module... Wait, the error appears when importing `xml.parsers.expat`? Let me check: python3 -c \"import xml.parsers.expat\" in the venv.\n\nThe system python3 worked earlier (probe scripts ran with system python). The venv python is the same interpreter... but venvs don't inherit site-packages, and pyexpat is part of the stdlib (built into the interpreter or in lib-dynload). Hmm, Ubuntu's python3.13 might have pyexpat in a separate deb (python3.13-minimal?) but the system python found it.\n\nWait — maybe the venv python is a different version (e.g. python3.13 from a different source). Let me check both.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794385912151,"end":1794385912173}}}
